WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.000
Despite "Barbie" being nominated for&nbsp;
Best Picture for the 2024 Academy Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.200
Margot Robbie wasn't nominated for Best Leading&nbsp;
Actress, and Greta Gerwig was shut out of Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:17.280
Director category. In the end, these snubs are&nbsp;
dispiriting, but utterly unsurprising. Here's why.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520
Year after year, the Best Picture race is&nbsp;
predominantly occupied by movies helmed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.960
men — white men, more often than not — with only a&nbsp;
few women breaking through to secure a nomination.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.600
It's important to note that, in&nbsp;
the Academy's 95-year history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.640
only three women have ever won the top prize:&nbsp;
Kathryn Bigelow for "The Hurt Locker" in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:42.320
Chloé Zhao for "Nomadland" in 2021, and Jane&nbsp;
Campion for "The Power of the Dog" in 2022.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.800
It's also important to say that,&nbsp;
at the upcoming 96th Oscars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.240
French director Justine Triet is the sole woman&nbsp;
recognized in the category, and last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.160
zero female directors were nominated.&nbsp;
This is an obvious and systemic issue!

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.760
Greta Gerwig is actually a pretty fascinating&nbsp;
example of a woman who frequently receives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.920
half-hearted, seemingly insincere honors from&nbsp;
the Academy without actual, tangible recognition.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.960
Yes, she was nominated for directing "Lady&nbsp;
Bird," but she lost to Guillermo del Toro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.600
for "The Shape of Water" and was up against only&nbsp;
men. Her sophomore effort, "Little Women," scored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.320
a Best Picture nod and ignored Gerwig's&nbsp;
considerable work from behind the camera.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.120
Now, with "Barbie," Gerwig has been shut&nbsp;
out for her third critically acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.865
film. The snub is egregious, but it's&nbsp;
also par for the course at this point.

00:01:29.865 --> 00:01:34.185
"Everything's almost, like, reversed here."

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.800
Margot Robbie is technically an Oscar nominee at

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.240
the 96th Academy Awards — but not for her&nbsp;
performance in "Barbie." As a producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.080
she earned a nomination for its&nbsp;
inclusion in the Best Picture category.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.440
This is, at face value, pretty shocking ...&nbsp;
especially when you consider that Robbie was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.560
perhaps, edged out by Carey Mulligan&nbsp;
in Bradley Cooper's biopic "Maestro",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.640
which was met with mixed reviews.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.840
Robbie does something fairly spectacular in&nbsp;
"Barbie," considering that she transforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.880
America's most famous doll into a&nbsp;
fully realized character. Robbie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.280
vision of Barbie — aided in no small part&nbsp;
by Greta Gerwig's excellent yet unrecognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.360
direction and the script she co-wrote with&nbsp;
her partner Noah Baumbach — takes her on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.480
journey that transforms her into a real&nbsp;
person, someone desperate to become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.120
one who imagines instead of being simply an&nbsp;
idea. In doing all of this, Robbie commits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.040
one of the biggest sins possible in the eyes&nbsp;
of the Motion Picture Academy — she's funny.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.480
"I don't have anything big planned,&nbsp;
just a giant, blow-out party with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.440
all the Barbies and planned choreography&nbsp;
and a bespoke song. You should stop by!"

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.640
Comedy is so consistently overlooked at&nbsp;
the Oscars that when Marisa Tomei won for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.720
her pitch-perfect performance&nbsp;
in "My Cousin Vinny" in 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.120
rumors swirled for years that presenter&nbsp;
Jack Palance read the wrong name.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.520
Robbie's portrayal is heartfelt, emotional, and&nbsp;
compelling, but she also goes for broad comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:59.400
showing off the full range of her acting ability&nbsp;
by combining pratfalls with tearful admissions.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.680
"I'm not used to that having anything in it!"

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.040
The Oscars rarely, if ever, reward comedy,&nbsp;
likely because it's not viewed as "serious"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.600
art compared to biopics or movies that&nbsp;
make people feel completely depressed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.760
Robbie's performance being ignored is yet&nbsp;
another example of that irritating trend.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.040
Those excusing "Barbie's" egregious omissions&nbsp;
in Best Director and Leading Actress might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.360
point to the fact that the movie broke multiple&nbsp;
box-office records and made Greta Gerwig the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.840
first female director to single-handedly helm&nbsp;
a movie that crossed the billion-dollar mark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.280
All of these accomplishments may have hurt&nbsp;
its full Oscar potential in the end, though.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.400
Blockbusters, like comedies, were staunchly&nbsp;
ignored by the Academy for most of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.600
history — until outcry over Christopher Nolan's&nbsp;
universally adored Batman take, "The Dark Knight,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.480
won a posthumous acting award for Heath Ledger&nbsp;
without managing a Best Picture nomination.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.080
From 2010 on, the Best Picture category expanded&nbsp;
to ten potential winners, usually tossing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.720
blockbuster into the fray to appease fans and&nbsp;
draw a bigger audience for the telecast. Movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.920
like "Black Panther," "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;
and "Mad Max: Fury Road," just to name a few,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.840
were never going to win Best Picture, but were&nbsp;
nominated to keep complaints to a minimum.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.040
"Barbie's" billion-dollar box-office haul&nbsp;
has relegated it to the "token blockbuster"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.080
role this year. This is made particularly obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.480
considering that it's nominated&nbsp;
alongside several smaller films.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.520
"Oppenheimer" was also a high-grossing&nbsp;
cinematic juggernaut in 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.520
but unlike "Barbie," "Oppenheimer" is&nbsp;
more likely to be regarded as "serious"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.280
because it's a historical biopic&nbsp;
about the creation of the atomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.720
bomb ... while "Barbie" apparently loses&nbsp;
artistic merit with each dollar it makes.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.200
"Barbie" ended up with Best Picture and&nbsp;
Best Adapted Screenplay nominations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.600
but it also scored two acting nods in the&nbsp;
supporting categories. America Ferrera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.760
who plays Mattel employee Gloria and&nbsp;
delivers the film's most memorable monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:50.760
earned her first-ever Oscar nomination&nbsp;
for supporting actress. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.040
Ryan Gosling ended up with a nomination&nbsp;
for supporting actor — which is a problem.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.120
Gosling's performance is, to be fair, truly&nbsp;
excellent. He shines in every single second of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.400
the film, committing to his role as the hapless,&nbsp;
horse-obsessed Ken with the utmost passion.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.960
"Horses are just men extenders."

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.400
"Ohhhh."

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.160
His "dream ballet," set to the&nbsp;
Oscar-nominated original song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.520
"I'm Just Ken," is certainly one&nbsp;
of the movie's standout moments.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.920
That said, it's a bit on the nose to&nbsp;
nominate Gosling and not Margot Robbie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.080
considering that one of the film's main conflicts&nbsp;
is that Ken overthrows the matriarchy of Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.760
Land to install a patriarchy and become&nbsp;
the society's "main character," of sorts.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.960
Nominating the only standout male character&nbsp;
from a movie about the female experience — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:39.400
not the film's lead — is, frankly, insulting,&nbsp;
as are the other major "Barbie" nominations.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.840
Unless it pulls a full "Argo," its&nbsp;
chances of winning Best Picture are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.800
utterly insignificant without Greta&nbsp;
Gerwig in the Best Director category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.360
Its screenplay nod feels like the&nbsp;
Academy giving a small consolation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.800
prize to a movie that feels too weird&nbsp;
or "unworthy" to win the big prize.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.720
Ultimately, "Barbie" being overlooked&nbsp;
in majorly important categories isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.080
all that shocking ... but it&nbsp;
does continue a disturbing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.173
unsettling trend of not taking movies seriously&nbsp;
if they're too funny, feminine, or fantastical.

